#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533761} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8c605a-0b0c-5f15-b9c1-1d00ad746eb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" :question/math-academy-id "q-127236" :question/problem "Find the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $f(4)=-5$ and ${f}^{′}(4)=-7,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5-7(x-4) \\\\  & =-5-7x+28 \\\\  & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533416} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e837d9-716d-555b-80cd-9638260052b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127237" :question/problem "Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $f(4.1).$\n\n$f(4.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(4.1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=4.1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1) & ≈L(4.1) \\\\  & =-7(4.1)+23 \\\\  & =-5.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533335} :answer-field/id #uuid "6abdcfbb-d03d-54ab-93d9-988a3323ce36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" :question/math-academy-id "q-127238" :question/problem "Write the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=3,$ we plug $a=3$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $f(3)=6,$ ${f}^{′}(3)=-5,$ and ${f}^{″}(3)=-6$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(-6)(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533641} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff2781d-f48f-5ca8-a7f3-24940603f906" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127239" :question/problem "Use the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 3 to estimate the value of $f(3.2).$\n\n$f(3.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our second-degree Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈{P}_{2}(3.2) \\\\  & =6-5(3.2-3)-3(3.2-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(0.2)-3(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =4.88.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6d2873-9a05-5246-b704-4e9d162b2b64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967" :question/math-academy-id "q-127240" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe value of ${f}^{″}(4)=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're given an expansion about the point $x=4,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(4)=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20824866-0419-512a-b241-e3eb66b30194" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Function Given Some Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^2-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^2-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533721} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d214404-722b-5c57-9c39-86b4d12c6c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127241" :question/problem "Write the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4.$\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2!}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{3!}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=-5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4)=-7 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4)=-10 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(4)=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor Polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-5-7(x-4)+\\frac{(-10)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(-13)}{6}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20824866-0419-512a-b241-e3eb66b30194" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Function Given Some Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533257} :answer-field/id #uuid "f443cc95-d040-59a2-a1e7-0a2ba700ead3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127242" :question/problem "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.9).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$f(3.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation is\n\n$$\n{P}_{3}(x)=-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.9)$ by evaluating the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9) & ≈{P}_{3}(3.9) \\\\  & =-5-7(3.9-4)-5(3.9-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(3.9-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(-0.1)-5(-0.1)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(-0.1)^{3} \\\\  & ≈-4.348.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533432} :answer-field/id #uuid "568b31fb-dca1-54e0-b91d-7f6b4c28406c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296" :question/math-academy-id "q-127243" :question/problem "According to the Lagrange error bound, the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=4$ approximates $f(3.9)$ with an error that is less than{{field-1}}\n(Do not round your answer, write it as a fraction)." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-4{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=4$ and $x=3.91.$ Plugging $x=3.9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3.9-4{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}(0.1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to find the maximum of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval $[3.9,4].$\n\nSince $f$ and all its derivatives are decreasing on the interval $[2,4],$ and since ${f}^{(4)}(x)$ is negative in this interval, we know that $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ is an increasing function on the interval $[3.9,4]$ and its value is greatest at $x=4.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(4)|=|-6|=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{6}{24}(0.1)^{4}=\\frac{1}{40\\,000}.\n$$"}]}
